#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166491 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166890 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8666dd9-b763-589d-8ae3-af203068f1a4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maximum Value Given a Canonical Form on the Unit Sphere"}] :question/id #uuid "b8306278-726a-5c09-86cc-14631a6abb66" :question/math-academy-id "e-12659" :question/problem "Find the maximum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x})=2x_1^2 - 5x_2^2 +3x_3^2$ given that $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $x_1^2 \\geq 0$ and $x_2^2 \\geq 0.$ So, we have that \n\n$$\n2x_1^2 \\leq 3x_1^2, \\qquad -5x_2^2 \\leq 3x_2^2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}-5{x}_{2}^{2}+3{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & \\le 3{x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}+3{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =3({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1$ is equivalent to $x_1^2 + x_2^2 + x_3^2 =1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2})=3(1)=3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\leq 3.$\n\nSo, the value of $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ can be no greater than $3.$ But before we conclude that $3$ is the maximum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(\\mathbf{x}) = 3.$\n\nNotice that when ![](images/c4/c4afaf37dc0b68f09e5efac6434f3f22bc8edc107f45ce7f3327c475f4158ea7.svg) we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}-5{x}_{2}^{2}+3{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =2(0)^{2}-5(0)^{2}+3(1)^{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1,$ the maximum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is equal to $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166492 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166891 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "33f7fa4d-9ac0-552a-9f3e-9e09c2ed0286" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Minimum Value Given a Canonical Form on the Unit Sphere"}] :question/id #uuid "5b4117f3-62fb-55c6-8e23-0cadcd4a2ec5" :question/math-academy-id "e-12660" :question/problem "Find the minimum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x})=5x_1^2 - 4x_2^2 - 6x_3^2$ given that $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $x_1^2 \\geq 0$ and $x_2^2 \\geq 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n5x_1^2 \\geq -6x_1^2, \\qquad -4x_2^2 \\geq -6x_2^2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =5{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{2}^{2}-6{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & \\ge -6{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{2}^{2}-6{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =-6({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1$ is equivalent to $x_1^2 + x_2^2 + x_3^2 =1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2})=-6(1)=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\geq -6.$\n\nSo, the value of $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ can be no smaller than $-6.$ But before we conclude that $-6$ is the minimum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(\\mathbf{x}) = -6.$\n\nNotice that when ![](images/cf/cfee6f101db09d50c60a1f539ebb116e5b3912ac0dfc1ee3fb646ecf7f3f6287.svg) we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =5{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{2}^{2}-6{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =5(0)^{2}-4(0)^{2}-6(1)^{2} \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1,$ the minimum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is equal to $-6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166493 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166892 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b2e9ac0-590b-587c-b16d-309f899c21e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maximum and Minimum Values of a Quadratic Form on the Unit Sphere"}] :question/id #uuid "3cec995b-8918-5c48-927e-b0807a947518" :question/math-academy-id "e-12661" :question/problem "Find the minimum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2-2x_2^2-2x_3^2+2x_2x_3$  given that $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\max \\{ Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: | \\:  \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}\n$$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\min \\{ Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: | \\:  \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n![](images/e2/e2f302904c9cd4126b12ef7c1839a6f4aa0f5f02fae8b1ab749bc2c2924eb584.svg)\n\nNow, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of the matrix $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )((-2-\\lambda )(-2-\\lambda )-1) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +3) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(\\lambda +3)(\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =1,\\,-1,\\,-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\min \\{ Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: | \\:  \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = -3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167098 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166891 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "33f7fa4d-9ac0-552a-9f3e-9e09c2ed0286" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Minimum Value Given a Canonical Form on the Unit Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46924927-06ec-56cb-98e1-4a9f8257bf59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266774} {:answer/id #uuid "8a6af117-5920-5cc7-ba0d-0d5bb48ed086" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267379} {:answer/id #uuid "a80d8439-2c6f-5e40-a229-93b535ff35b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267665} {:answer/id #uuid "f03bd214-43d7-5b50-bb06-16406a90f3a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268281} {:answer/id #uuid "f0d9261b-1e2a-5411-931c-6dd0970c4046" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268286}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46924927-06ec-56cb-98e1-4a9f8257bf59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266774} :answer-field/id #uuid "89fbe449-3952-54bf-9b43-6ba33b308a80" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268652}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0706b1eb-0c66-56aa-86af-d962b1e95896" :question/math-academy-id "q-136824" :question/problem "Find the minimum value of $Q(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}-{x}_{3}^{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${x}_{1}^{2}\\ge 0$ and ${x}_{3}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n2{x}_{1}^{2}\\ge -3{x}_{1}^{2},\\,-{x}_{3}^{2}\\ge -3{x}_{3}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}-{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & \\ge -3{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}-3{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =-3({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $‖x‖=1$ is equivalent to ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}=1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2})=-3(1)=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(x)\\ge -3.$\n\nSo, the value of $Q(x)$ can be no smaller than $-3.$ But before we conclude that $-3$ is the minimum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(x)=-3.$\n\nNotice that when $x={[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}-{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =2(0)^{2}-3(1)^{2}-(0)^{2} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that $‖x‖=1,$ the minimum value of $Q(x)$ is equal to $-3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167099 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166892 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b2e9ac0-590b-587c-b16d-309f899c21e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maximum and Minimum Values of a Quadratic Form on the Unit Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56933511-7bac-56df-b79c-e43f47de1424" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378686} {:answer/id #uuid "af549cc0-3f08-5b96-911b-8266acb72440" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378687} {:answer/id #uuid "1ae28847-626b-5c4b-84eb-5668493198b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378688} {:answer/id #uuid "6616269d-d8b0-5e02-b6cc-b20f0191ea43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378689} {:answer/id #uuid "4d29e524-5968-5c11-92b5-924629837ad0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378690}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56933511-7bac-56df-b79c-e43f47de1424" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378686} :answer-field/id #uuid "693f5701-baa5-5230-8d27-8fbfe6ab9c1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378685}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cdc523d0-2005-5617-9a5b-100488432c13" :question/math-academy-id "q-136825" :question/problem "Find the maximum value of $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of the matrix $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167101 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166891 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "33f7fa4d-9ac0-552a-9f3e-9e09c2ed0286" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Minimum Value Given a Canonical Form on the Unit Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bb2b3ec-b506-57b1-a321-ef34d500b312" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266380} {:answer/id #uuid "3fb34a44-ccc4-5d93-a0ee-d1a270a49f96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266719} {:answer/id #uuid "7ce773be-46a8-5749-9f3c-45e0184f84dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267250} {:answer/id #uuid "950803c3-f359-5b8b-854f-fac51f406e73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267475} {:answer/id #uuid "a9a6f32c-3450-50b7-a94c-f53570e5d4d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267675}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1bb2b3ec-b506-57b1-a321-ef34d500b312" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266380} :answer-field/id #uuid "3fae4afb-886b-5f96-b0c8-2b5e66121cf1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268513}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "657b8539-11db-5516-89dc-20b2cb407f63" :question/math-academy-id "q-138019" :question/problem "Find the minimum value of $Q(x)=5{x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${x}_{1}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n5{x}_{1}^{2}\\ge 3{x}_{1}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =5{x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & \\ge 3{x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =3({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $‖x‖=1$ is equivalent to ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2})=3(1)=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(x)\\ge 3.$ \n\nSo, the value of $Q(x)$ can be no smaller than $3.$ But before we conclude that $3$ is the minimum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(x)=3.$\n\nNotice that when  $x={[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =5{x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =5(0)+3(1)^{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that $‖x‖=1,$ the minimum value of $Q(x)$ is equal to $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269160 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166890 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8666dd9-b763-589d-8ae3-af203068f1a4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maximum Value Given a Canonical Form on the Unit Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d6f64ea-5883-59b6-87d8-0c414835bf9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266397} {:answer/id #uuid "64bf79c6-9bd3-58b2-a03f-31ea6d676ad2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267035} {:answer/id #uuid "6835f060-12f4-5f7c-800f-2d11804b0999" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267068} {:answer/id #uuid "75d606cc-7006-5c54-8b0d-87575a052797" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267183} {:answer/id #uuid "8b01eb48-f505-5230-9bfc-e142c70b0dcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267391}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b01eb48-f505-5230-9bfc-e142c70b0dcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267391} :answer-field/id #uuid "28c921d2-404f-547b-a4f2-1796fd2b6c1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268470}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4b3d9103-b2fd-58c5-b20f-fd57d948503c" :question/math-academy-id "q-138021" :question/problem "Find the maximum value of $Q(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{2}^{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${x}_{2}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n-4{x}_{2}^{2}\\le 2{x}_{2}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & \\le 2{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =2({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $‖x‖=1$ is equivalent to ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2})=2(1)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(x)\\le 2.$\n\nSo, the value of $Q(x)$ can be no greater than $2.$ But before we conclude that $2$ is the maximum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(x)=2.$\n\nNotice that when $x={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =2(1)^{2}-4(0)^{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, given that $‖x‖=1,$ the maximum value of $Q(x)$ is equal to $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269161 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166890 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8666dd9-b763-589d-8ae3-af203068f1a4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maximum Value Given a Canonical Form on the Unit Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a2b5512-df42-581a-b353-3fabc43cee3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266235} {:answer/id #uuid "97b2abc9-31d1-5823-9bc4-76f1d7b7e754" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267501} {:answer/id #uuid "9d61cd04-19aa-52dd-b6e1-03d9829033b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267560} {:answer/id #uuid "a1f07942-a1d9-5001-83ad-0233540581f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267600} {:answer/id #uuid "df0b9433-e3c9-59d0-a9f8-afcc07ddf9d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268115}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d61cd04-19aa-52dd-b6e1-03d9829033b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267560} :answer-field/id #uuid "73d457e3-1350-5f7d-95f8-cb4b91e459e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268622}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "af1b3d3b-8379-544e-8bfe-948c68812b5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-138023" :question/problem "Find the maximum value of $Q(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}-3{x}_{3}^{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${x}_{3}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n-3{x}_{3}^{2}\\le 2{x}_{3}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}-3{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & \\le 2{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =2({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $‖x‖=1$ is equivalent to ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}=1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2})=2(1)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(x)\\le 2.$\n\nSo, the value of $Q(x)$ can be no greater than $2.$ But before we conclude that $2$ is the maximum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(x)=2.$\n\nNotice that when $x={[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}-3{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =2(0)^{2}+2(1)^{2}-3(0)^{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that $‖x‖=1,$ the maximum value of $Q(x)$ is equal to $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167102 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166890 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8666dd9-b763-589d-8ae3-af203068f1a4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maximum Value Given a Canonical Form on the Unit Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9516beb9-455a-5cc4-acd9-2f4aab580daf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163691} {:answer/id #uuid "c776ec1f-1f89-5eeb-a6a6-cd6e0d0b73bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163692} {:answer/id #uuid "d4320d75-9d5d-55be-92cd-dd228b2b92d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163693} {:answer/id #uuid "4cc2b2ca-b114-5175-bbf3-2a0a05281d23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163694} {:answer/id #uuid "55147a9e-8df5-5ec2-8af3-14a446fc7cd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163695}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4cc2b2ca-b114-5175-bbf3-2a0a05281d23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163694} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f9417e7-ccc2-5461-bbb5-dcd957763f91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163690}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "104289cf-8277-54fc-85de-4e1b2c51d42a" :question/math-academy-id "q-138024" :question/problem "Find the maximum value of $Q(x)=4{x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{3}^{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${x}_{2}^{2}\\ge 0$ and ${x}_{3}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n3{x}_{2}^{2}\\le 4{x}_{2}^{2},\\,2{x}_{3}^{2}\\le 4{x}_{3}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =4{x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & \\le 4{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =4({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $‖x‖=1$ is equivalent to ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}=1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2})=4(1)=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(x)\\le 4.$ \n\nSo, the value of $Q(x)$ can be no greater than $4.$ But before we conclude that $4$ is the maximum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(x)=4.$\n\nNotice that when $x={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =4{x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =4(1)^{2}+3(0)^{2}+2(0)^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that $‖x‖=1,$ the maximum value of  $Q(x)$ is equal to $4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269165 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166891 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "33f7fa4d-9ac0-552a-9f3e-9e09c2ed0286" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Minimum Value Given a Canonical Form on the Unit Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "153b1cc1-f1b8-5692-a555-aa83d2319c1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266324} {:answer/id #uuid "1ad4911c-bca4-53c3-a9c7-19d389a73796" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266371} {:answer/id #uuid "5633496f-0847-5ac2-8354-19bbb38416c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266912} {:answer/id #uuid "f6b02623-7d38-5701-b7a5-be9d5aecb396" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268330} {:answer/id #uuid "f8b14a00-455b-5248-9a67-9f02bd5571df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268348}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "153b1cc1-f1b8-5692-a555-aa83d2319c1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266324} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6a67f1a-a4d6-5410-a486-f5c067d11840" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268839}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "12200f9b-72b6-5817-8209-f85aa2b1e6f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-138065" :question/problem "Find the minimum value of $Q(x)=-3{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{2}^{2}+5{x}_{3}^{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${x}_{1}^{2}\\ge 0$ and ${x}_{3}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n-3{x}_{1}^{2}\\ge -4{x}_{1}^{2},\\,5{x}_{3}^{2}\\ge -4{x}_{3}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =-3{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{2}^{2}+5{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & \\ge -4{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{2}^{2}-4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =-4({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $‖x‖=1$ is equivalent to ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}=1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2})=-4(1)=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(x)\\ge -4.$   \n\nSo, the value of $Q(x)$ can be no smaller than $-4.$ But before we conclude that $-4$ is the minimum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(x)=-4.$\n\nNotice that when $x={[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =-3{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{2}^{2}+5{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =-3(0)^{2}-4(1)^{2}+5(0)^{2} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that $‖x‖=1,$ the minimum value of $Q(x)$ is equal to $-4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269168 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166892 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b2e9ac0-590b-587c-b16d-309f899c21e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maximum and Minimum Values of a Quadratic Form on the Unit Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "233cbc8c-e991-527f-9870-8237e9259827" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266464} {:answer/id #uuid "56e3fb50-941c-5071-8184-b8b5efbbc951" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266917} {:answer/id #uuid "75092e81-eb62-517d-a2e7-5d3e74d2d302" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267174} {:answer/id #uuid "86732239-f564-5d20-a6f0-287cc9127ae5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267329} {:answer/id #uuid "c2215b72-50a3-58e0-b851-56494e72a6ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267875}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2215b72-50a3-58e0-b851-56494e72a6ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267875} :answer-field/id #uuid "c559be84-8136-5eb8-a035-4f4b66f98088" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268758}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "87883ff5-3c1f-5958-9ae9-a0cb7b8aff97" :question/math-academy-id "q-138068" :question/problem "Find the maximum value of $Q(x)=5{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+5{x}_{2}^{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of the matrix $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +21 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)(\\lambda -7) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}=7.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269170 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166892 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b2e9ac0-590b-587c-b16d-309f899c21e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maximum and Minimum Values of a Quadratic Form on the Unit Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04ff6694-ed52-501e-9c74-482fc606115b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266189} {:answer/id #uuid "4ac22209-68a0-5ff5-87ab-24d903edf82a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266807} {:answer/id #uuid "69d1b0b5-a3c7-5bd0-b0ea-07afbce368e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267082} {:answer/id #uuid "8ca167eb-75e4-5231-9e14-28b84b7d28c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267401} {:answer/id #uuid "8cb91b01-0006-51fd-a05f-946591dc8083" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267402}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04ff6694-ed52-501e-9c74-482fc606115b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266189} :answer-field/id #uuid "65b848da-440e-5f9d-8e85-bf37dd01b592" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268593}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a6c7fbad-0cbf-556a-b0c9-e63c761b749b" :question/math-academy-id "q-138070" :question/problem "Find the minimum value of $Q(x)=3{x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of the matrix $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )(3-\\lambda )-1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +8 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)(\\lambda -4) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =2,\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378698 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166892 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b2e9ac0-590b-587c-b16d-309f899c21e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maximum and Minimum Values of a Quadratic Form on the Unit Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10e8fb69-9f44-54d0-bd05-804474fc28c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378700} {:answer/id #uuid "72b442c4-0af9-57a0-b12e-baf37ccd0d65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378701} {:answer/id #uuid "32608b6c-5259-57f3-8578-4b5368a63121" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378702} {:answer/id #uuid "bd8fafa8-4dce-5f8a-9c7d-8a2dae1db255" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378703} {:answer/id #uuid "186dce86-a68d-5764-9403-e470d7793588" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378704}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "186dce86-a68d-5764-9403-e470d7793588" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378704} :answer-field/id #uuid "b79cb6ac-c413-5f59-b2f9-d516aeede4f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378699}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0773c5be-04a4-5953-bd31-ab2b0d9b1c6a" :question/math-academy-id "q-138071" :question/problem "Find the minimum value of $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}-{x}_{3}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of the matrix $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-9) & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -8) & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )(\\lambda -4)(\\lambda +2) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =4,\\,-1,\\,-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}=-2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269172 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166892 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b2e9ac0-590b-587c-b16d-309f899c21e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maximum and Minimum Values of a Quadratic Form on the Unit Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bc61afe-1f09-5c43-9bcf-9f7a93b45d19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266254} {:answer/id #uuid "0dfe0c04-fca0-5511-91ac-85b8a6eb9a5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266267} {:answer/id #uuid "241dfe50-c27b-570b-a643-a7ca66632117" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266473} {:answer/id #uuid "907366c7-6c93-5a0f-b134-cc843d610cf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267430} {:answer/id #uuid "ff5c9ea6-44b8-585f-a1dc-edfd28c5628a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268395}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff5c9ea6-44b8-585f-a1dc-edfd28c5628a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268395} :answer-field/id #uuid "da8e03a6-145a-59ae-adee-a94e649f904e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268794}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7affa7e5-177d-56c4-b158-cb7cb8121dd7" :question/math-academy-id "q-138075" :question/problem "Find the  maximum value of $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{3}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of the matrix $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-4) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,2,\\,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278197 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166890 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8666dd9-b763-589d-8ae3-af203068f1a4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maximum Value Given a Canonical Form on the Unit Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0038ddda-e455-5e22-b133-45f5afd6bfa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275481} {:answer/id #uuid "4c6fe5fb-e999-5dc9-a386-3749054aca39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276151} {:answer/id #uuid "7ad233a9-13ef-5aca-a1bd-8edf1a1dd256" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276591} {:answer/id #uuid "926a1bae-a807-5bfe-b855-f0f51c9d9e7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276801} {:answer/id #uuid "e7e28dbb-2254-50d6-bde2-6c9cabe1d0bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277506}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0038ddda-e455-5e22-b133-45f5afd6bfa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275481} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b630154-06fe-530e-bfba-fa83ff574f2b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277845}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e74167c4-c7fe-5e65-910c-e5deae2cc1bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-158526" :question/problem "Find the maximum value of $Q(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}-5{x}_{3}^{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${x}_{2}^{2}\\ge 0$ and ${x}_{3}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n-3{x}_{2}^{2}\\le 2{x}_{2}^{2},\\,-5{x}_{3}^{2}\\le 2{x}_{3}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}-5{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & \\le 2{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =2({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $‖x‖=1$ is equivalent to ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}=1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2})=2(1)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(x)\\le 2.$\n\nSo, the value of $Q(x)$ can be no greater than $2.$ But before we conclude that $2$ is the maximum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(x)=2.$\n\nNotice that when $x={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}-5{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =2(1)^{2}-3(0)^{2}-5(0)^{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that $‖x‖=1,$ the maximum value of $Q(x)$ is equal to $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378759 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166890 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8666dd9-b763-589d-8ae3-af203068f1a4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maximum Value Given a Canonical Form on the Unit Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c051e1e-9e72-5d6d-82b8-f28b68533602" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378761} {:answer/id #uuid "af95651a-de2f-555f-ae3c-e83ff3491dfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378762} {:answer/id #uuid "c8108d8b-bcce-577a-994e-6f67d119c1c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378763} {:answer/id #uuid "71e06af8-fa30-564b-8609-7dd83e64cbcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378764} {:answer/id #uuid "10bfe917-e56f-5915-a08d-592cd912ac79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378765}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8108d8b-bcce-577a-994e-6f67d119c1c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378763} :answer-field/id #uuid "69041f32-949e-5bd8-9f4b-53eadb70896e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378760}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "639b5fe1-808a-5fa4-9b00-50f2df78b358" :question/math-academy-id "q-158527" :question/problem "Find the maximum value of $Q(x)=3{x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{3}^{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${x}_{1}^{2}\\ge 0$ and ${x}_{3}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n3{x}_{1}^{2}\\le 6{x}_{1}^{2},\\,4{x}_{3}^{2}\\le 6{x}_{3}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =3{x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & \\le 6{x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}+6{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =6({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $‖x‖=1$ is equivalent to ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}=1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2})=6(1)=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(x)\\le 6.$ \n\nSo, the value of $Q(x)$ can be no greater than $6.$ But before we conclude that $6$ is the maximum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(x)=6.$\n\nNotice that when $x={[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =3{x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =3(0)^{2}+6(1)^{2}+4(0)^{2} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that $‖x‖=1,$ the maximum value of  $Q(x)$ is equal to $6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492028 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166891 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "33f7fa4d-9ac0-552a-9f3e-9e09c2ed0286" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Minimum Value Given a Canonical Form on the Unit Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7af27e76-63ba-50ec-8eb2-b883774e51ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491669} {:answer/id #uuid "7b2ba24e-ff58-5939-98b8-c0a4ae606ce5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491671} {:answer/id #uuid "89eb46f6-972b-5dce-a398-209e4aac862e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491689} {:answer/id #uuid "d3b19c16-3291-5a56-87dc-4937a4b60eb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491814} {:answer/id #uuid "f4486f88-56ea-5beb-bb11-91eb5a7aa4e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491871}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7af27e76-63ba-50ec-8eb2-b883774e51ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491669} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed2b6ea2-7bc6-53de-a437-5cb53c04fb85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491977}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "77d3128f-57d6-56c8-a326-3ba5851a500a" :question/math-academy-id "q-158530" :question/problem "Find the minimum value of $Q(x)=-2{x}_{1}^{2}-5{x}_{2}^{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${x}_{1}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n-2{x}_{1}^{2}\\ge -5{x}_{1}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =-2{x}_{1}^{2}-5{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & \\ge -5{x}_{1}^{2}-5{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =-5({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $‖x‖=1$ is equivalent to ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=1.$ So,  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2})=-5(1)=-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(x)\\ge -5.$\n\nSo, the value of $Q(x)$ can be no smaller than $-5.$ But before we conclude that $-5$ is the minimum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(x)=-5.$\n\nNotice that when $x={[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =-2{x}_{1}^{2}-5{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =-2(0)^{2}-5(1)^{2} \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that $‖x‖=1,$ the minimum value of $Q(x)$ is equal to $-5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492073 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166891 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "33f7fa4d-9ac0-552a-9f3e-9e09c2ed0286" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Minimum Value Given a Canonical Form on the Unit Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1eb4aba8-ac20-5cbd-be43-99fed3db4846" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491519} {:answer/id #uuid "2c883ac6-45f3-5671-8996-be49b1f4a1fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491544} {:answer/id #uuid "68330fbe-d8f3-5d83-9652-1a55a837aea6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491641} {:answer/id #uuid "8c90b13a-d0a3-5b8a-8698-21fda41d9626" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491693} {:answer/id #uuid "a57f100c-02ec-5d7d-9c50-3f1638cc0828" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491741}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c90b13a-d0a3-5b8a-8698-21fda41d9626" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491693} :answer-field/id #uuid "704abb49-2936-53d0-b0de-fa6cfab24dbc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491925}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fce93d41-81b4-5cb3-80dd-626cc0b50873" :question/math-academy-id "q-158535" :question/problem "Find the minimum value of $Q(x)=-5{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{3}^{2}$ given that $‖x‖=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${x}_{2}^{2}\\ge 0$ and ${x}_{3}^{2}\\ge 0.$ So, we have that\n\n$$\n-3{x}_{2}^{2}\\ge -5{x}_{2}^{2},\\,2{x}_{3}^{2}\\ge -5{x}_{3}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =-5{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & \\ge -5{x}_{1}^{2}-5{x}_{2}^{2}-5{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =-5({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $‖x‖=1$ is equivalent to ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}=1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2})=-5(1)=-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand, in turn, $Q(x)\\ge -5.$   \n\nSo, the value of $Q(x)$ can be no smaller than $-5.$ But before we conclude that $-5$ is the minimum, we need to find a vector such that $Q(x)=-5.$\n\nNotice that when $x={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =-5{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =-5(1)^{2}-3(0)^{2}+2(0)^{2} \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, given that $‖x‖=1,$ the minimum value of $Q(x)$ is equal to $-5.$"}]}
